<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Entitlements for the Mac App Store build. Five keys, and every one of them
  earns its place.

  The App Store build is compiled with no build tag at all, which is what
  leaves Sparkle out of the binary: the tag is -tags sparkle and it adds the
  updater rather than removing it, so forgetting it yields a build with no
  updater instead of a submission carrying one. None of Sparkle's requirements
  appear here as a result. That is deliberate and load-bearing:
  com.apple.security.temporary-exception.* in a submission is scrutinised
  closely, and a temporary exception whose purpose is self-updating is a direct
  read on guideline 2.4.5. There is no version of this file that both ships
  Sparkle and survives review.

  Keep double hyphens out of these comments; see entitlements.plist.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
    <true/>

    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- WKWebView refuses to run without this in a sandboxed app, even though
         the frontend is embedded in the binary and served over the wails://
         custom scheme, and even though this build opens no sockets of its own.
         WebKit's helper processes are launched by the host and inherit its
         sandbox; the Networking process cannot start without outgoing
         connections, and WebContent then refuses too:

           WebContent[...] Application does not have permission to communicate
           with network resources. rc=1 : errno=34
           WebProcessProxy::processDidTerminateOrFailedToLaunch: reason=Crash

         Nothing else reports a problem. The app launches, the tray icon
         appears, clipboard capture and its notifications keep working, and the
         popup opens as an empty panel because the web view behind it never
         rendered a frame. Only a sandboxed run shows it, which is why the
         Developer ID build, which carries this key for Sparkle, never did.

         App Privacy is unaffected: no data leaves the machine. See
         build/appstore/metadata.md, which says so in the terms review asks. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- Both must match the provisioning profile exactly, team prefix and all,
         or the upload is rejected before review ever sees it. Replace 88BTYX26S4
         with the Team ID from developer.apple.com Membership details. -->
    <key>com.apple.application-identifier</key>
    <string>88BTYX26S4.com.geda.clipboard</string>
    <key>com.apple.developer.team-identifier</key>
    <string>88BTYX26S4</string>
</dict>
</plist>
